Repeatedly asking for another example is not itself progress. Before requesting a demo, record the live hypotheses and identify what property of a new input-output pair would separate them. Prefer a deliberately informative variation in a still-unknown parameter—period, phase, orientation, palette, motif size, or marker placement—rather than an arbitrary fresh example. After each pair, check whether it resolves that parameter and whether the remaining ambiguity could change the test output. Stop requesting once the transformation and test-specific parameters are adequately supported; if the demos do not identify those parameters, keep the uncertainty explicit rather than implying confidence from demo volume.
